WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.199
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C one, Book three.

2
00:00:05.499 --> 00:00:08.932
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.232 --> 00:00:15.785
Unit twenty-two: History of Medicine. Today you will credit medical discoveries with the passive + agent.

4
00:00:16.085 --> 00:00:18.598
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.533 --> 00:00:22.813
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:23.650 --> 00:00:24.500
Medicine.

7
00:00:25.517 --> 00:00:28.817
Medicine means the science of treating and preventing disease.

8
00:00:29.417 --> 00:00:32.508
Medicine advanced rapidly in the 19th century.

9
00:00:33.117 --> 00:00:35.479
Your turn. Say: Medicine.

10
00:00:38.333 --> 00:00:39.276
Medicine.

11
00:00:40.017 --> 00:00:41.076
Disease.

12
00:00:42.083 --> 00:00:45.592
Disease means an illness affecting people, animals or plants.

13
00:00:46.183 --> 00:00:48.520
Malaria remains a serious disease.

14
00:00:49.117 --> 00:00:51.606
Your turn. Say: Disease.

15
00:00:54.467 --> 00:00:55.456
Disease.

16
00:00:56.217 --> 00:00:57.322
Epidemic.

17
00:00:58.317 --> 00:01:01.396
Epidemic means a rapid spread of disease in a community.

18
00:01:01.983 --> 00:01:04.482
A cholera epidemic struck the city.

19
00:01:05.083 --> 00:01:07.956
Your turn. Say: Epidemic.

20
00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:11.801
Epidemic.

21
00:01:12.550 --> 00:01:13.470
Vaccine.

22
00:01:14.483 --> 00:01:18.166
Vaccine means a substance that gives immunity against a disease.

23
00:01:18.750 --> 00:01:21.295
The vaccine was developed by Edward Jenner.

24
00:01:21.883 --> 00:01:24.269
Your turn. Say: Vaccine.

25
00:01:27.133 --> 00:01:28.320
Vaccine.

26
00:01:29.083 --> 00:01:30.003
Hygiene.

27
00:01:31.017 --> 00:01:34.560
Hygiene means practices that keep people clean and healthy.

28
00:01:35.150 --> 00:01:37.382
Better hygiene reduced infections.

29
00:01:37.983 --> 00:01:40.345
Your turn. Say: Hygiene.

30
00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:44.062
Hygiene.

31
00:01:44.817 --> 00:01:45.876
Public health.

32
00:01:46.883 --> 00:01:50.590
Public health means efforts to protect the health of whole populations.

33
00:01:51.183 --> 00:01:53.543
Clean water is a public health priority.

34
00:01:54.150 --> 00:01:57.185
Your turn. Say: Public health.

35
00:02:00.033 --> 00:02:01.092
Public health.

36
00:02:01.850 --> 00:02:02.584
Treatment.

37
00:02:03.583 --> 00:02:06.198
Treatment means medical care given to a patient.

38
00:02:06.783 --> 00:02:09.363
Artemisinin is the main treatment for malaria.

39
00:02:09.950 --> 00:02:12.579
Your turn. Say: Treatment.

40
00:02:15.433 --> 00:02:16.249
Treatment.

41
00:02:16.983 --> 00:02:18.031
Progress.

42
00:02:19.017 --> 00:02:21.841
Progress means development towards a better condition.

43
00:02:22.450 --> 00:02:25.193
Progress against polio has been remarkable.

44
00:02:25.783 --> 00:02:28.575
Your turn. Say: Progress.

45
00:02:31.433 --> 00:02:32.481
Progress.

46
00:02:33.267 --> 00:02:36.906
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:37.750 --> 00:02:40.864
Visal, who do we thank for the smallpox vaccine?

48
00:02:41.950 --> 00:02:54.828
The first vaccine was developed by Edward Jenner in 1796, although inoculation had already been practised by healers in Asia and Africa.

49
00:02:55.917 --> 00:02:58.520
And how was smallpox finally eliminated?

50
00:02:59.617 --> 00:03:09.279
The disease was eradicated by a global vaccination campaign led by the World Health Organization, completed in 1980.

51
00:03:10.383 --> 00:03:12.302
Any Cambodian connection?

52
00:03:13.417 --> 00:03:22.989
Yes. Resistance to malaria drugs was first detected by researchers in western Cambodia, which alerted the world to a dangerous threat.

53
00:03:24.133 --> 00:03:25.529
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:26.383 --> 00:03:28.917
Grammar focus: Passive with agent.

55
00:03:29.217 --> 00:03:30.369
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:31.083 --> 00:03:34.209
The smallpox vaccine was developed by Edward Jenner.

57
00:03:35.117 --> 00:03:38.788
Smallpox was eradicated by a global vaccination campaign.

58
00:03:39.683 --> 00:03:43.192
Drug resistance was first detected by researchers in Cambodia.

59
00:03:44.133 --> 00:03:54.379
Use 'by + agent' to give credit for a discovery or achievement. Adverbs such as first, later and finally go between 'was' and the past participle: 'was first detected by…'.

60
00:03:55.117 --> 00:03:59.441
Be careful! Don't say: The vaccine was developed from Jenner.

61
00:04:01.400 --> 00:04:04.201
Say: The vaccine was developed by Jenner.

62
00:04:04.501 --> 00:04:05.978
Use 'by' for the agent.

63
00:04:06.767 --> 00:04:09.303
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:10.100 --> 00:04:11.867
From Miasma to Microbes.

65
00:04:12.167 --> 00:04:17.894
For most of history, disease was explained by theories of bad air, imbalance or divine punishment.

66
00:04:18.900 --> 00:04:24.940
In the nineteenth century, germ theory was established by scientists such as Louis Pasteur and Robert Koch.

67
00:04:25.933 --> 00:04:31.497
Public health was transformed by clean water systems, which dramatically reduced epidemics of cholera.

68
00:04:32.500 --> 00:04:40.920
Antibiotics, discovered by Alexander Fleming in 1928, later turned deadly infections into treatable conditions.

69
00:04:41.933 --> 00:04:48.031
Yet progress has been uneven, and many diseases are still worsened by poverty and unequal access to care.

70
00:04:49.133 --> 00:04:53.193
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:54.050 --> 00:04:56.549
Penicillin blank by Alexander Fleming.

72
00:04:56.849 --> 00:05:00.990
A, discovered. B, was discovered. C, has discover.

73
00:05:04.533 --> 00:05:06.742
The answer is B: was discovered.

74
00:05:07.883 --> 00:05:11.148
The vaccine was developed blank a team of scientists.

75
00:05:11.448 --> 00:05:14.777
A, by. B, from. C, with by.

76
00:05:18.333 --> 00:05:20.240
The answer is A: by.

77
00:05:21.383 --> 00:05:24.672
A rapid spread of disease in a community is an blank.

78
00:05:24.972 --> 00:05:29.577
A, epidemic. B, hygiene. C, vaccine.

79
00:05:33.133 --> 00:05:34.912
The answer is A: epidemic.

80
00:05:36.050 --> 00:05:38.061
Which collocation is correct?

81
00:05:38.361 --> 00:05:44.244
A, eradicate a disease. B, erase up a disease. C, kill out a disease.

82
00:05:47.800 --> 00:05:50.786
The answer is A: eradicate a disease.

83
00:05:51.950 --> 00:05:55.331
According to the reading, how was public health transformed?

84
00:05:55.631 --> 00:06:00.538
A, by clean water systems. B, by bad air. C, by divine punishment.

85
00:06:04.100 --> 00:06:06.750
The answer is A: by clean water systems.

86
00:06:07.933 --> 00:06:11.062
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:11.917 --> 00:06:14.950
Which medical advance do you consider most important?

88
00:06:31.750 --> 00:06:33.227
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:33.527 --> 00:07:10.681
I consider vaccination the most important medical advance in history. The first vaccine was developed by Edward Jenner, and since then diseases such as smallpox and polio have been almost completely defeated. In Cambodia, vaccination programmes have greatly reduced child deaths over the past thirty years. Others might argue that antibiotics are more important, since they cure so many infections. However, prevention is better than cure, and vaccines protect whole communities at a very low cost.

90
00:07:10.981 --> 00:07:12.701
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:13.617 --> 00:07:17.476
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and passive with agent.

92
00:07:17.776 --> 00:07:22.008
Today's value is gratitude. Say thank you for your home and family.

93
00:07:22.308 --> 00:07:26.017
See you in Unit twenty-three. Goodbye!

94
00:07:26.317 --> 00:07:29.375
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

